-The production measurements are on ten queries over a private corpus and are reported as aggregates the reader cannot check. They establish that the failure happened and how it looked, not an effect size, and the claim that the keyword retriever had been inert since the system's first version is an inference from unchanged code.
+The production measurements used here are on ten queries over a private corpus and are reported as aggregates the reader cannot check. They establish that the failure happened and how it looked, not an effect size, and the claim that the keyword retriever had been inert since the system's first version is an inference from unchanged code.
 
 The public reproduction uses six collections, three base-sized 768-dimensional encoders, and FTS5's default BM25 weighting of title and text. The three encoders rerun the same queries, so the 18 pairs are not 18 independent samples, and the paired tests are corrected within each family of 18 but not across the six families; corrected over all 108 tests at once, 23 of the 31 differences significant within their families remain so, among them all seven significant comparisons of the repaired hybrid with the dense retriever at nDCG@10. The collections differ in domain, corpus size, document length, relevance style and query length at once, so the orderings reported here (empty rate against content tokens per query, short-list disjointness across collections of different size) are observations over five or six points, not controlled effects, and the pooled row-count rates mix the two builders, whose short lists arise differently. The truncation sweep covers only the two smallest collections.
 
